<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Arsuf</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Arsuf is the site of an ancient and medieval town on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a> coast north of modern Herzliya, Israel, known in antiquity as Apollonia. Taken by the crusaders in 1101, it was the seat of a lordship of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-jerusalem">Kingdom of Jerusalem</a>, and in 1191 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/richard-i-of-england">Richard I</a> defeated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saladin">Saladin</a> nearby. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mamluk">Mamluk</a> sultan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baybars">Baybars</a> captured and destroyed it in 1265, and its ruins form Apollonia National Park.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/arsuf.jpg" alt="Arsuf" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: אור פ · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Arsur_Fortress1.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
